"```python\n# Model Level Entities\nclass User(db.Models):\n    def get_present_name(self):\n        # Database level entity: What kind of data does my application store?\n        # The user's name is stored in the database\n        return self.name or 'Anonymous'\n\n    def activate(self):\n        # Application level entity: What does my application do?\n        # Activate the user's account\n        self.status = 'activated'\n        self.save()\n        # Send a confirmation email to the user\n        send_mail('Your account is activated!', '\u2026', [self.email])\n\n# View Level Entities\nclass UserView(db.Models):\n    def get_present_name(self):\n        # Database level entity: What kind of data does my application store?\n        # The user's name is stored in the database\n        return User.objects.get(uid=self.uid).name or 'Anonymous'\n\n    def activate(self):\n        # Application level entity: What does my application do?\n        # Activate the user's account\n        User.objects.get(uid=self.uid).status = 'activated'\n        User.objects.get(uid=self.uid).save()\n        # Send a confirmation email to the user\n        send_mail('Your account is activated!', '\u2026', [self.email])\n```\n\nIn this example, we've separated the database level entities (e.g., `User` model) from the application level entities (e.g., `UserView` model). This separation of concerns makes the code more maintainable, scalable, and easier to understand. Each level of the application has its own responsibility, making it easier to control and debug."
