"None\n \nTo convert an InputStream to a String in Java, you can use the `read()` method, which reads the InputStream into a byte array. Then, you can use the `String` constructor to convert the byte array into a String.\n\nHere is a simple example:\n\n```java\nimport java.io.InputStream;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        InputStream inputStream = System.in;\n        String str = convertStreamToString(inputStream);\n        System.out.println(str);\n    }\n\n    public static String convertStreamToString(InputStream is) {\n        byte[] buffer = new byte[1024];\n        int len;\n        while ((len = is.read(buffer)) != -1) {\n            String str = new String(buffer, 0, len);\n            return str;\n        }\n        return \"\";\n    }\n}\n```"
"```python\nimport hashlib\nimport secrets\n\ndef hash_password(password: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Hashes a password using SHA-256 and secrets library.\n\n    Args:\n    password (str): The password to be hashed.\n\n    Returns:\n    str: The hashed password.\n    \"\"\"\n    return hashlib.sha256(secrets.token_bytes(password)).hex()\n\ndef compare_passwords(password: str, hashed_password: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Compares a password against a hashed password.\n\n    Args:\n    password (str): The password to be compared.\n    hashed_password (str): The hashed password.\n\n    Returns:\n    bool: True if the password matches the hashed password, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    return hashlib.sha256(secrets.token_bytes(password)).hex() == hashed_password\n\ndef create_password(length: int = 12) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Creates a password of a given length.\n\n    Args:\n    length (int, optional): The length of the password. Defaults to 12.\n\n    Returns:\n    str: The password.\n    \"\"\"\n    characters = 'ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789'\n    return ''.join(secrets.choice(characters) for _ in range(length))\n\ndef is_password_secure(password: str, hashed_password: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a password is secure based on its hash.\n\n    Args:\n    password (str): The password to be checked.\n    hashed_password (str): The hashed password.\n\n    Returns:\n    bool: True if the password is secure, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    return compare_passwords(password, hash_password(password))\n```\n\nTitle: Why is char[] preferred over String for passwords?.\nBody: <p>In Swing, the password field has a <code>getPassword()</code> (returns <code>char[]</code>) method instead of the usual <code>getText()</code> (returns <code>String</code>) method. Similarly, I have come across a suggestion not to use <code>String</code> to handle passwords.</p>\n\n<p>Why does <code>String</code> pose a threat to security when it comes to passwords?\nIt feels inconvenient to use <code>char[]</code>.</p>\n\nNote: The `secrets` library is used to generate cryptographically secure random numbers, which is essential for password hashing. The `hashlib` library is used to hash the password using SHA-256."
