"```python\nimport pandas as pd\n\ndef pretty_print_series(df):\n    \"\"\"\n    Pretty-prints a pandas Series.\n\n    Args:\n        df (pandas.Series): The Series to pretty-print.\n\n    Returns:\n        str: A pretty-printed string representation of the Series.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Use the to_string method to print the Series\n    print(df.to_string(index=False, header=True, justify='right', min_width=80))\n\ndef pretty_print_dataframe(df):\n    \"\"\"\n    Pretty-prints a pandas DataFrame.\n\n    Args:\n        df (pandas.DataFrame): The DataFrame to pretty-print.\n\n    Returns:\n        str: A pretty-printed string representation of the DataFrame.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Use the to_string method to print the DataFrame\n    return df.to_string(index=False, header=True, justify='right', min_width=80)\n\n# Create a sample Series\ns = pd.Series([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n\n# Pretty-print the Series\nprint(\"Pretty-printing Series:\")\npretty_print_series(s)\n\n# Create a sample DataFrame\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    'A': [1, 2, 3],\n    'B': [4, 5, 6],\n    'C': [7, 8, 9]\n})\n\n# Pretty-print the DataFrame\nprint(\"\\nPretty-printing DataFrame:\")\npretty_print_dataframe(df)\n```\n\nThis code defines two functions, `pretty_print_series` and `pretty_print_dataframe`, which take a pandas Series or DataFrame as input and return a pretty-printed string representation of the data. The `pretty_print_series` function uses the `to_string` method to print the Series, while the `pretty_print_dataframe` function uses the `to_string` method to print the DataFrame. Both functions include comments to explain their purpose and usage."
"```python\nimport pandas as pd\n\ndef dict_to_dataframe(dict_list):\n    \"\"\"\n    Convert a list of dictionaries into a pandas DataFrame.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    dict_list (list): A list of dictionaries where each dictionary represents a row in the DataFrame.\n    \n    Returns:\n    pandas.DataFrame: A pandas DataFrame representing the input list of dictionaries.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    # Initialize an empty list to store the dictionaries as rows\n    df_dict_list = []\n    \n    # Iterate over each dictionary in the input list\n    for dictionary in dict_list:\n        # Extract the required columns from the dictionary\n        month = dictionary.get('month', 'Unknown')\n        points = dictionary.get('points', 0)\n        points_h1 = dictionary.get('points_h1', 0)\n        \n        # Create a new DataFrame row with the extracted values\n        row = {\n            'month': month,\n            'points': points,\n            'points_h1': points_h1,\n            'time': dictionary.get('time', 'Unknown'),\n            'year': dictionary.get('year', 0)\n        }\n        \n        # Append the row to the list of rows\n        df_dict_list.append(row)\n    \n    # Create a pandas DataFrame from the list of rows\n    df = pd.DataFrame(df_dict_list)\n    \n    return df\n\n# Example usage\ndict_list = [{'points': 50, 'time': '5:00', 'year': 2010}, \n             {'points': 25, 'time': '6:00', 'month': 'february'}, \n             {'points':90, 'time': '9:00', 'month': 'january'}, \n             {'points_h1':20, 'month': 'june'}]\ndf = dict_to_dataframe(dict_list)\nprint(df)\n```"
