"implements Runnable vs extends Thread\nFrom what time I've spent with threads in Java, I've found these two ways to write threads:\n\nThe primary difference between implementing `Runnable` and extending `Thread` lies in the way you can create and start instances of these classes.\n\nWhen you implement `Runnable`, you can create a new instance of your class like this:\n\n```java\nMyRunnable myRunnable = new MyRunnable();\n```\n\nAnd start it like this:\n\n```java\nmyRunnable.run();\n```\n\nOn the other hand, when you extend `Thread`, you create a new class that already has the `run()` method defined, so you don't need to implement anything:\n\n```java\npublic class MyThread extends Thread {\n    public MyThread() {\n        super(\"MyThread\");\n    }\n    public void run() {\n        //Code\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis is more concise and easier to read, especially when you have a lot of code to put into the `run()` method. However, extending `Thread` has some limitations - you can't override any of the methods from the `Thread` class, and you can't use the `start()` method."
