"```csharp\npublic class Order\n{\n    public int OrderId { get; set; }\n    public DateTime OrderDate { get; set; }\n    public int Quantity { get; set; }\n    public decimal Total { get; set; }\n\n    public Order(int orderId, DateTime orderDate, int quantity, decimal total)\n    {\n        OrderId = orderId;\n        OrderDate = orderDate;\n        Quantity = quantity;\n        Total = total;\n    }\n}\n\npublic class Program\n{\n    public static List<Order> SortOrdersByProperty(List<Order> objListOrder, string propertyName)\n    {\n        if (propertyName == \"OrderId\")\n        {\n            objListOrder.Sort((x, y) => x.OrderId.CompareTo(y.OrderId));\n        }\n        else if (propertyName == \"OrderDate\")\n        {\n            objListOrder.Sort((x, y) => x.OrderDate.CompareTo(y.OrderDate));\n        }\n        else\n        {\n            throw new ArgumentException(\"Invalid property name\");\n        }\n\n        return objListOrder;\n    }\n\n    public static void GetOrderList(List<Order> objListOrder)\n    {\n        foreach (var order in objListOrder)\n        {\n            Console.WriteLine($\"OrderId: {order.OrderId}, OrderDate: {order.OrderDate}, Quantity: {order.Quantity}, Total: {order.Total}\");\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void Main()\n    {\n        List<Order> objListOrder = new List<Order>();\n        objListOrder.Add(new Order(1, new DateTime(2022, 1, 1), 10, 100.00m));\n        objListOrder.Add(new Order(2, new DateTime(2022, 1, 2), 20, 200.00m));\n        objListOrder.Add(new Order(3, new DateTime(2022, 1, 3), 30, 300.00m));\n\n        SortOrdersByProperty(objListOrder, \"OrderId\");\n        SortOrdersByProperty(objListOrder, \"OrderDate\");\n    }\n}\n```"
"Big O notation is a way to describe the performance or complexity of an algorithm. It's a measure of how long an algorithm takes to complete, or how much memory it uses, as the size of the input increases.\n\nFor example, consider a function that checks if an array contains a specific element. The time complexity would be O(n), because the function needs to iterate over each element in the array. This means that as the size of the array increases, the time it takes to check for the element will increase linearly.\n\nOn the other hand, consider a function that checks if an array contains a specific element and has a built-in method like `in` that does this in constant time (i.e., O(1)). This means that as the size of the array increases, the time it takes to check for the element will remain the same, regardless of how large the array is.\n\nBig O notation is used to compare the performance of different algorithms and to choose the best one for a given problem. It's a way to say, \"This algorithm will take this amount of time to complete as the size of the input increases.\""
