"None\nWhen to use IMG vs. CSS background-image:\n \n- Use IMG tags for static images where the image's dimensions and layout are fixed, such as in an HTML table or a simple layout. This is because IMG tags are supported in older browsers and have better rendering performance compared to CSS background-image.\n- Use CSS background-image for dynamic images where the image's dimensions and layout change based on the content of the surrounding HTML elements, such as in a responsive design or when the image is loaded dynamically. This is because CSS background-image has better support for animations and transitions, and can be used to create complex layouts and effects.\n \nNote: In modern web development, using IMG tags for dynamic content is generally discouraged due to its limitations and potential performance issues. It's recommended to use CSS background-image instead."
"None\n\nTo change the fill color of an SVG element using CSS, you need to add the `fill` attribute to the SVG element and set its value. In this case, you want to change the fill color from white to black, so you would use `fill: #000;`.\n\nHere's the corrected code:\n\n<pre><code>&lt;img src=\"logo.svg\" alt=\"Logo\" class=\"logo-img\"&gt;\n  &lt;style&gt;\n    .logo-img { fill: #000; }\n  &lt;/style&gt;\n</code></pre>\n\nThis code will apply the `fill: #000;` style to the `.logo-img` class, changing the fill color of the SVG image to black."
