"None\nTo clear the value of an input file control using jQuery, you can use the `attr()` method with the `value` attribute and set it to an empty string. However, this approach only works if the file input control is a direct child of the element with the id `control`. \n\nIf the file input control is a descendant of the element with the id `control`, you would need to get the element that contains the file input control and then clear its value. \n\nHere is an example of how you can achieve this:\n\n```javascript\n$('#control').on('change', function() {\n  var fileInput = $(this).closest('input[type=\"file\"]');\n  if (fileInput.length > 0) {\n    fileInput.attr('value', '');\n  }\n});\n```\n\nThis code attaches a change event listener to the file input control. When the file input control changes, it gets the closest input type \"file\" element (i.e., the file input control) and clears its value. \n\nHowever, if you are using a more complex setup with multiple input controls, this might not work as expected. In such cases, you would need to get the element that contains the file input control and then clear its value."
"```python\nimport json\n\ndef get_form_data(form):\n    \"\"\"\n    Get the form data as a dictionary.\n\n    Args:\n        form (dict): The form data.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The form data as a dictionary.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Initialize an empty dictionary to store the form data\n    data = {}\n\n    # Iterate over each field in the form\n    for field in form:\n        # Get the field name and value\n        field_name = field['name']\n        field_value = field['value']\n\n        # Add the field name and value to the data dictionary\n        data[field_name] = field_value\n\n    # Return the data dictionary\n    return data\n\n# Example usage:\nform = {\n    'foo': '1',\n    'bar': 'xxx',\n    'this': 'hi'\n}\n\nform_data = get_form_data(form)\nprint(json.dumps(form_data, indent=4))\n```"
"None\nYou can use the `readonly` parameter in the form fields to make them read-only. Here's an example:\n\n```python\nclass Item(models.Model):\n    sku = models.CharField(max_length=50)\n    description = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    added_by = models.ForeignKey(User)\n\nclass ItemForm(ModelForm):\n    readonly_fields = ('sku',)\n    class Meta:\n        model = Item\n        exclude = ('added_by',)\n\ndef new_item_view(request):\n    if request.method == 'POST':\n        form = ItemForm(request.POST)\n        # Validate and save\n    else:\n        form = ItemForm()\n    # Render the view\n```\n\nIn the `ItemForm` class, you can add the `readonly_fields` parameter to specify the fields that should be read-only. In this example, the `sku` field is read-only."
