"**JavaScript Closures Explained**\n\nA closure is a fundamental concept in JavaScript programming that allows a function to have access to its own scope, as well as the scope of its outer functions. When a function is defined inside another function, it creates a closure over the variables of the outer function.\n\nThink of it like a pair of gloves. The outer function is like the first glove, and the inner function is like the second glove. Even though the inner function is defined inside the outer function, it still has access to the variables of the outer function, because those variables are still \"on\" when the inner function is defined. This is similar to how the inner function can access the variables of the outer function, even though they are no longer accessible from outside the closure.\n\nHere's a simple example to illustrate this concept:\n```javascript\nfunction outer() {\n  let x = 10;\n\n  function inner() {\n    console.log(x); // prints 10\n  }\n\n  inner();\n}\n\nouter();\n```\nIn this example, the `outer` function defines a variable `x` and a nested function `inner`. The `inner` function has access to the variable `x`, even though it's defined inside the `outer` function. This is a closure, because the `inner` function is able to access the variables of the `outer` function, even though they are no longer accessible from outside the closure.\n\nThis concept is useful for a variety of purposes, such as creating private variables or functions that can access the state of the surrounding scope."
