"```python\ndef make_bold(func):\n    def wrapper():\n        return \"<b>\" + func() + \"</b>\"\n    return wrapper\n\ndef make_italic(func):\n    def wrapper():\n        return \"<i>\" + func() + \"</i>\"\n    return wrapper\n\n@make_bold\n@make_italic\ndef say():\n    return \"Hello\"\n\nprint(say())\n```\n\nIn this code, `make_bold` and `make_italic` are two decorators that wrap the `say` function and apply the styles to it. The `@` symbol before the decorator function is used to indicate that it should be applied to the `say` function. The `say` function is called twice, once for each decorator, and the returned values are combined to form the final output."
