"None\n\nThe most reliable way to check if a variable has been initialized is by using the `is` keyword or the `typeof` operator. Here's why:\n\n- `is` checks for object identity, which is true only when the variable points to the same object as the variable on the right-hand side. If the variable is reassigned, it will still point to the same object. This is not the same as checking if the variable is defined or initialized.\n- `typeof elem` checks if the variable `elem` is of a certain type (e.g., string, number, object, etc.). If the variable is not of that type, `typeof elem` will return a string indicating the type of the variable.\n- `elem != null` checks if the variable `elem` is not null. This is not a reliable way to check if the variable has been initialized, as it can be overridden by assigning a value to `elem` later.\n\nIn summary, using `is` or `typeof` is the safest way to check if a variable has been initialized, as they provide more robust and accurate results than `elem != null`."
"```javascript\n// Variables inside a function\nfunction example() {\n    let x = 10; // Local variable\n    function outer() {\n        let y = 20; // Local variable, outside the function\n        console.log(x); // Accessing the local variable\n        console.log(y);\n    }\n    outer();\n}\n\n// Variables defined globally\nlet x = 10; // Global variable\nfunction outer() {\n    let y = 20; // Local variable, outside the function\n    console.log(x); // Accessing the global variable\n    console.log(y);\n}\n\nexample();\n```\n\nThis will output:\n\n```\n10\n20\n10\n20\n```\n\nIn JavaScript, variables declared inside a function have the same scope as the function itself. This means that if a variable is declared inside a function, it will only be accessible within that function and any functions that call it. \n\nIn contrast, variables declared outside a function have the same scope as the global scope. This means that they can be accessed from anywhere in the program, and can also be accessed from within a function."
