"None\n \nTo pass all arguments passed to your Bash script to your Python function, you can use the `sys` module which allows you to access command line arguments. Here's an example of how you can modify your Python function to accept all command line arguments:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef abc(*args):\n    # Get all command line arguments\n    all_args = sys.argv\n    \n    # Check if all required arguments are present\n    required_args = ['a', 'b', 'c']\n    if not all(arg in all_args for arg in required_args):\n        print(\"Error: Not all required arguments are passed.\")\n        return\n    \n    # Process the arguments\n    print(\"Received arguments:\")\n    for i, arg in enumerate(all_args):\n        print(f\"{i+1}: {arg}\")\n    \n    # Do something with the arguments\n    print(\"Doing something with the arguments...\")\n    \n    return None\n```"
