"None\nYou can use the optional chaining operator (?.) in JavaScript to simplify optional parameter handling. The operator returns the second operand if the first operand is not null or undefined. Here's how you can use it:\n\n```javascript\nfunction myFunc(requiredArg, optionalArg) {\n  const defaultValue = 'defaultValue';\n  return optionalArg?.concat(defaultValue);\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `optionalArg?.concat(defaultValue)` will return `'defaultValue'` if `optionalArg` is not null or undefined, and will return `undefined` if it is. This approach is more concise and expressive than the original `||` operator."
