"None\n \nThe use of internal access modifier in C# can be useful for controlling access to certain types or assemblies, but it's not always the best choice for unit testing. \n\nWhen to use internal:\n\n*   You want to test a specific assembly or type that is not intended to be used outside the project.\n*   You want to isolate testing code from other code that might be modifying the internal data.\n*   You're using a testing library that requires internal access to work correctly.\n\nWhen not to use internal:\n\n*   You're writing unit tests for public-facing APIs or services.\n*   You're using a testing library that doesn't require internal access.\n*   You're working on a project with multiple testing projects, and you want to avoid duplicating testing code. \n\nIn general, if you're testing a specific type or assembly, internal access might be a good choice. However, if you're writing unit tests for public-facing APIs or services, or if you're working on a project with multiple testing projects, private access might be a better option."
