"import git\n\ndef download_folder(repo_url, folder_name):\n    # Initialize the Git client\n    repo = git.Repo(repo_url)\n\n    # Check if the specified folder exists in the repository\n    if folder_name in repo.index().tree.bare:\n        # Get the path to the folder\n        folder_path = repo.index().tree.bare + folder_name\n\n        # Download the folder\n        repo.git.fetch()\n        repo.git.clone_tree(folder_path)\n    else:\n        print(f\"The folder {folder_name} does not exist in the repository.\")\n\n# Example usage\nrepo_url = \"git@github.com:foobar/Test.git\"\nfolder_name = \"foo\"\ndownload_folder(repo_url, folder_name)"
"```java\nimport java.io.BufferedReader;\nimport java.io.FileReader;\nimport java.io.IOException;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static String readFile(String file) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(file));\n        StringBuilder stringBuilder = new StringBuilder();\n        String lineSeparator = System.getProperty(\"line.separator\");\n\n        try {\n            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {\n                stringBuilder.append(line).append(lineSeparator);\n            }\n        } finally {\n            reader.close();\n        }\n\n        return stringBuilder.toString();\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String filePath = \"path_to_your_file.txt\"; // replace with your file path\n        String content = readFile(filePath);\n        System.out.println(content);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis code reads the contents of a file into a string using the `readFile` method, which is a more concise and efficient way to achieve this task compared to the original idiom."
