"```python\n# Understanding Python super() with __init__() methods\n\n# Why is super() used?\n# super() is used to call the parent class's __init__() method from a child class.\n# It provides a way to access the parent class's methods and variables from the child class.\n# This is especially useful when you need to initialize the child class with some data or call the parent class's method.\n\n# Is there a difference between using Base.__init__ and super().__init__?\n# There is no difference. Both are used to call the parent class's __init__() method.\n# However, the first method requires the parent class to explicitly define __init__() method,\n# while the second method is more Pythonic and allows the parent class to define its own __init__() method.\n```"
