"None\n\nWhen it comes to data structures in C++, both `struct` and `class` are used to define custom data types. However, there are key differences between the two that make one more suitable for certain scenarios than the other.\n\nIn general, `struct` is a better choice when you need to define a simple, lightweight data type with a fixed set of members. It's ideal for representing a single, well-defined entity with a minimal number of fields. `struct` is similar to a C-style struct, and it's often used for this purpose.\n\nOn the other hand, `class` is a more versatile option when you need to define a complex data type with multiple fields, methods, and behavior. It's perfect for representing a class of objects with a rich set of attributes and operations. `class` is similar to a C++ class, and it's often used for this purpose.\n\nHere are some scenarios where `struct` might be a better choice:\n\n*   When you need to represent a single, well-defined entity with a minimal number of fields.\n*   When you're working with C-style structures, where you need to define a lightweight data type with a fixed set of members.\n*   When you want to use the `struct` syntax to define a simple, intuitive data type.\n\nOn the other hand, `class` might be a better choice when you need to define a complex data type with multiple fields, methods, and behavior. Here are some scenarios where `class` might be a better choice:\n\n*   When you want to represent a class of objects with a rich set of attributes and operations.\n*   When you need to define a custom data type with inheritance, polymorphism, and other object-oriented features.\n*   When you're working with C++ classes, where you need to use the `class` syntax to define a complex, object-oriented data type.\n\nIn summary, `struct` is a good choice when you need a simple, lightweight data type with a fixed set of members, while `class` is a better choice when you need a complex data type with multiple fields, methods, and behavior."
"```python\n# Old Style Classes\nclass OldStyleClass:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n    def print_name(self):\n        print(self.name)\n\n    def new_method(self):\n        print(\"This is a new method\")\n\n# New Style Classes\nclass NewStyleClass:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n        self.attributes = []\n\n    def add_attribute(self, attribute):\n        self.attributes.append(attribute)\n\n    def get_attributes(self):\n        return self.attributes\n\n    def new_method(self):\n        print(\"This is a new method\")\n\n# Creating instances\nold_style_instance = OldStyleClass(\"John\")\nnew_style_instance = NewStyleClass(\"Alice\")\n\n# Using old style methods\nold_style_instance.print_name()\nold_style_instance.new_method()\n\n# Using new style methods\nnew_style_instance.print_name()\nnew_style_instance.new_method()\nnew_style_instance.add_attribute(\"Age\")\nnew_style_instance.get_attributes()\n```"
