"```python\n# In .NET, struct and class are two fundamental building blocks of object-oriented programming.\n# While they share some similarities, they have distinct differences in terms of their purpose, usage, and behavior.\n\n# A struct is a lightweight, value-type data container that represents a collection of fields.\n# It is similar to a Python tuple or a JavaScript object literal.\n# Structs are useful for representing simple data structures that do not require methods or inheritance.\n\n# A class, on the other hand, is a more complex data type that represents a class of objects.\n# It has its own set of fields (attributes) and methods (functions) that can be used to manipulate and interact with the objects.\n# Classes are useful for representing complex data structures, encapsulating behavior, and promoting inheritance.\n\n# In terms of syntax, the main difference between struct and class is the use of the `struct` keyword.\n# To create a struct in .NET, you use the `struct` keyword followed by the name of the struct.\n# To create a class, you use the `class` keyword followed by the name of the class.\n\n# Here's an example to illustrate the difference:\n```\n# Struct example\nstruct Person {\n    public string Name { get; set; }\n    public int Age { get; set; }\n}\n\n# Class example\npublic class Person {\n    public string Name { get; set; }\n    public int Age { get; set; }\n}\n```\n\n# In summary, while both struct and class are used for representing data structures in .NET, they differ in terms of their purpose, usage, and behavior.\n# Structs are lightweight, simple data containers, whereas classes are more complex, encapsulated data types with their own set of fields and methods."
