"```python\nclass CompanyInfo:\n    \"\"\"\n    A class used to store company information.\n\n    Attributes:\n        companyName (str): The company name.\n        companyAddress (str): The company address.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # Static class to store company information\n    static COMPANY_INFO = CompanyInfo\n\n    def __init__(self, companyName, companyAddress):\n        # Initialize the class with company information\n        self.companyName = companyName\n        self.companyAddress = companyAddress\n\n    # Static method to get company information\n    @staticmethod\n    def getCompanyName():\n        # Return the company name\n        return CompanyInfo.COMPANY_INFO.companyName\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def getCompanyAddress():\n        # Return the company address\n        return CompanyInfo.COMPANY_INFO.companyAddress\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        # Return a string representation of the company information\n        return f\"Company Name: {self.companyName}\\nCompany Address: {self.companyAddress}\"\n\n# Example usage\ncompany_info = CompanyInfo.getCompanyName()\nprint(company_info)\n```"
"None\n \n An abstract class in Java is a class that cannot be instantiated directly and is intended to be inherited by other classes. It has at least one abstract method (a method declared without an implementation) and provides a way to define the common base class for other classes.\n\nIn your example, you're trying to instantiate an abstract class and call its method. This is not allowed in Java. The `my` class is an abstract class because it has an abstract method `mymethod()`. When you try to instantiate `my`, the compiler will throw an error because you can't instantiate an abstract class directly.\n\nThe `poly` class is a class that contains an instance of the abstract class `my` and calls its method. This is allowed because `poly` is not an abstract class, it's a class that contains an instance of an abstract class. This is known as composition, where an object of a class contains an instance of another class.\n\nIn your case, you're trying to call the abstract method `mymethod()` on the instance of the abstract class `my`. This is not allowed because the `mymethod()` method is declared in the abstract class `my`, but it's not implemented in the abstract class. You need to implement this method in the subclass or in a separate class that implements the abstract class."
"```python\nclass Foo:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._done = False\n\n    def doSomething(self) -> None:\n        self._done = True\n        print(\"Something done\")\n\n    def _lock(self) -> None:\n        print(\"Lock acquired\")\n\n# Usage\nlock = Foo()\nlock._lock()\nlock.doSomething()\nlock._lock()\n```"
"import heapq\n\ndef find_pairs(n):\n    # Create a hash map to store the pairs\n    pairs = {}\n\n    # Iterate over the pairs\n    for i in range(n):\n        # Get the pair\n        pair = (i // 2, i // 2)\n\n        # If the pair is already in the hash map, skip it\n        if pair in pairs:\n            continue\n\n        # Add the pair to the hash map\n        pairs[pair] = None\n\n    # Initialize the result queue\n    result = []\n\n    # Iterate over the pairs\n    for pair in pairs:\n        # Get the pair\n        pair = pair\n\n        # If the pair is not in the result queue, add it\n        if pair not in result:\n            result.append(pair)\n\n    return result\n\nprint(find_pairs(30))"
