"```python\ndef sort_dict_by_key(dictionary):\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts a dictionary by its keys.\n\n    Args:\n        dictionary (dict): The dictionary to be sorted.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The sorted dictionary.\n    \"\"\"\n    return dict(sorted(dictionary.items()))\n\n# Example usage:\nmy_dict = {2: 3, 1: 89, 4: 5, 3: 0}\nsorted_dict = sort_dict_by_key(my_dict)\nprint(sorted_dict)\n```"
"```python\ndef sort_array_by_integers(array):\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts an array of integers correctly by extracting the integer values and sorting them.\n    \n    Args:\n        array (list): A list of integers.\n    \n    Returns:\n        list: A sorted list of integers.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Extract integer values from the array\n    integers = [num for num in array if isinstance(num, int)]\n    \n    # Sort the integers in ascending order\n    sorted_integers = sorted(integers)\n    \n    # Combine the sorted integers with the original array\n    sorted_array = integers + [num for num in array if isinstance(num, int)]\n    \n    return sorted_array\n\n# Example usage\narray = [140000, 104, 99]\nsorted_array = sort_array_by_integers(array)\nprint(sorted_array)\n```\n\nNote: This code uses a list comprehension to extract the integer values from the array and a sorted function to sort them. The sorted function is used to sort the integers in ascending order. The original array is then combined with the sorted integers to produce the final sorted array."
"```python\ndef sort_array_by_order(array, order_column):\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts a multi-dimensional array by the value of the 'order' key.\n\n    Args:\n        array (list): The input array.\n        order_column (int): The index of the 'order' key in each sub-array.\n\n    Returns:\n        list: The sorted array.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Use the sorted function with a custom key function that returns the order value\n    # The sorted function is stable, so when multiple records have the same key, \n    # their original order is preserved\n    return sorted(array, key=lambda sub_array: sub_array[order_column])\n\n# Example usage:\narray = [\n    [0] =&gt; Array\n        (\n            [hashtag] =&gt; a7e87329b5eab8578f4f1098a152d6f4\n            [title] =&gt; Flower\n            [order] =&gt; 3\n        )\n\n    [1] =&gt; Array\n        (\n            [hashtag] =&gt; b24ce0cd392a5b0b8dedc66c25213594\n            [title] =&gt; Free\n            [order] =&gt; 2\n        )\n\n    [2] =&gt; Array\n        (\n            [hashtag] =&gt; e7d31fc0602fb2ede144d18cdffd816b\n            [title] =&gt; Ready\n            [order] =&gt; 1\n        )\n]\n\nsorted_array = sort_array_by_order(array, 1)\nprint(sorted_array)\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```python\n[\n    [0], \n    [1], \n    [2]\n]\n```\n\nThis code defines a function `sort_array_by_order` that takes a multi-dimensional array and an integer `order_column` as input. The function uses the built-in `sorted` function with a custom key function to sort the array by the value of the 'order' key. The key function is a lambda function that returns the order value from each sub-array. The sorted function is stable, so when multiple records have the same key, their original order is preserved."
"None\n```python\nimport datetime\n\nclass Person:\n    def __init__(self, birthDate):\n        self.birthDate = birthDate\n\ndef sort_array_by_date(array):\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts an NSMutableArray of Person objects by birthDate.\n\n    Args:\n        array (NSMutableArray): The NSMutableArray of Person objects to sort.\n\n    Returns:\n        NSMutableArray: The sorted NSMutableArray of Person objects.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Use the sorted function with a custom key\n    sorted_array = sorted(array, key=lambda person: person.birthDate)\n    return sorted_array\n\n# Example usage:\nperson1 = Person(datetime.date(1990, 1, 1))\nperson2 = Person(datetime.date(1991, 1, 1))\nperson3 = Person(datetime.date(1992, 1, 1))\n\narray = [person1, person2, person3]\nsorted_array = sort_array_by_date(array)\n\n# Print the sorted array\nfor person in sorted_array:\n    print(person.birthDate)\n```"
"```java\nimport java.time.LocalDate;\nimport java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.Collections;\nimport java.util.Comparator;\n\npublic class CustomComparator {\n    public boolean compare(Object object1, Object object2) {\n        return ((LocalDate) object1).before((LocalDate) object2);\n    }\n}\n\npublic class RandomName {\n    private ArrayList<CustomObject> database;\n\n    public RandomName() {\n        this.database = new ArrayList<>();\n        // Initialize database with custom objects\n    }\n\n    public void addCustomObject(CustomObject customObject) {\n        database.add(customObject);\n    }\n\n    public void sortDatabaseByStartDate() {\n        Collections.sort(database, new CustomComparator());\n    }\n}\n\nclass CustomObject {\n    private LocalDate startDate;\n\n    public CustomObject(LocalDate startDate) {\n        this.startDate = startDate;\n    }\n\n    public LocalDate getStartDate() {\n        return startDate;\n    }\n}\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        RandomName randomName = new RandomName();\n        randomName.addCustomObject(new CustomObject(LocalDate.of(2022, 1, 1)));\n        randomName.addCustomObject(new CustomObject(LocalDate.of(2022, 2, 2)));\n        randomName.addCustomObject(new CustomObject(LocalDate.of(2022, 3, 3)));\n\n        System.out.println(\"Before sorting:\");\n        printDatabase(randomName.database);\n\n        randomName.sortDatabaseByStartDate();\n\n        System.out.println(\"After sorting:\");\n        printDatabase(randomName.database);\n    }\n\n    public static void printDatabase(ArrayList<CustomObject> database) {\n        for (CustomObject customObject : database) {\n            System.out.println(customObject.getStartDate());\n        }\n    }\n}\n```"
"```python\ndef sort_by_attribute(attribute_name, sort_order, list_of_objects):\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts a list of objects based on a specific attribute of each object.\n\n    Args:\n        attribute_name (str): The name of the attribute to sort by.\n        sort_order (str): The order of sorting. Can be 'asc' for ascending or 'desc' for descending.\n        list_of_objects (list): The list of objects to sort.\n\n    Returns:\n        list: The sorted list of objects.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Use the sorted function with a custom key to sort the list\n    sorted_list = sorted(list_of_objects, key=lambda obj: getattr(obj, attribute_name), reverse=(sort_order == 'desc'))\n\n    return sorted_list\n\n# Example usage:\nclass Tag:\n    def __init__(self, name, count):\n        self.name = name\n        self.count = count\n\n# Create a list of Tag objects\ntags = [Tag('toe', 10), Tag('leg', 2), Tag('ankle', 5)]\n\n# Sort the list by 'count' in descending order\nsorted_tags = sort_by_attribute('count', 'desc', tags)\n\n# Print the sorted list\nfor tag in sorted_tags:\n    print(tag.name, tag.count)\n```\n\nThis code defines a function `sort_by_attribute` that takes three arguments: `attribute_name` (the name of the attribute to sort by), `sort_order` (the order of sorting, either 'asc' or 'desc'), and `list_of_objects` (the list of objects to sort). It uses the `sorted` function with a custom key to sort the list based on the specified attribute. The `getattr` function is used to dynamically retrieve the attribute from each object, and the `lambda` function is used to specify the attribute to sort by. The `reverse` argument is used to sort in descending order. The example usage demonstrates how to create a list of `Tag` objects and sort it using the `sort_by_attribute` function."
