"None\n\nTo get the current date in JavaScript, you can use the `Date` object, which provides methods to retrieve the current date and time. Here is an example of how to do it:\n\n```javascript\nlet currentDate = new Date();\nconsole.log(currentDate.getDate());  // Gets the day of the month (1-31)\nconsole.log(currentDate.getMonth() + 1);  // Gets the month (0-11)\nconsole.log(currentDate.getFullYear());  // Gets the year (4-digit)\nconsole.log(currentDate.getDate() + '-' + (currentDate.getMonth() + 1) + '-' + currentDate.getFullYear());  // Gets the date in the format 'YYYY-MM-DD'\n```\n\nYou can also use template literals to format the date in a more readable way:\n\n```javascript\nlet currentDate = new Date();\nconsole.log(`Today's date is: ${currentDate.getDate()} ${currentDate.getMonth() + 1} ${currentDate.getFullYear()}`);\n```\n\nThese examples will output the current date in the format 'YYYY-MM-DD' or 'DD-MM-YYYY'."
"```javascript\nfunction compareDates(date1, date2) {\n    // Get the year, month and day from the input dates\n    let year1 = parseInt(date1.split('-')[0]);\n    let month1 = parseInt(date1.split('-')[1]);\n    let day1 = parseInt(date1.split('-')[2]);\n\n    let year2 = parseInt(date2.split('-')[0]);\n    let month2 = parseInt(date2.split('-')[1]);\n    let day2 = parseInt(date2.split('-')[2]);\n\n    // Check if both dates are in the past\n    if (year1 < year2 && month1 < month2 && day1 < day2) {\n        return \"The values are not in the past.\";\n    }\n    // Check if date1 is greater than date2\n    else if (year1 < year2 && month1 < month2 && day1 > day2) {\n        return \"Date1 is greater than date2.\";\n    }\n    // Check if date1 is less than date2\n    else if (year1 > year2 && month1 > month2 && day1 < day2) {\n        return \"Date1 is less than date2.\";\n    }\n    // If none of the above conditions are met, it means date1 and date2 are not in the past\n    else {\n        return \"Both dates are not in the past.\";\n    }\n}\n\n// Get the dates from the text boxes\nlet date1 = document.getElementById('date1').value;\nlet date2 = document.getElementById('date2').value;\n\n// Compare the dates and display the result\nconsole.log(compareDates(date1, date2));\n```\n\nYou can enter the dates in the format MM/DD/YYYY and then click on the \"Compare\" button to see the result."
