"import cv2\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef calculate_color_histogram(image):\n    # Convert image to HSV\n    hsv = cv2.cvtColor(image, cv2.COLOR_BGR2HSV)\n\n    # Define the range of colors to consider\n    lower_red = np.array([0, 100, 100])\n    upper_red = np.array([20, 255, 255])\n\n    # Threshold the HSV image to get only red colors\n    mask = cv2.inRange(hsv, lower_red, upper_red)\n\n    # Calculate the color histogram\n    hist = np.histogram(mask, 256, [0, 180])  # 256 bins for 0 to 180 degrees\n\n    return hist\n\ndef compare_images(image1, image2):\n    # Calculate the color histograms of both images\n    hist1 = calculate_color_histogram(image1)\n    hist2 = calculate_color_histogram(image2)\n\n    # Calculate the average color histograms\n    avg_hist1 = np.mean(hist1, axis=0)\n    avg_hist2 = np.mean(hist2, axis=0)\n\n    # Calculate the similarity using the Euclidean distance\n    distance = np.sqrt(np.sum((avg_hist1 - avg_hist2) ** 2))\n\n    # Return the similarity value\n    return distance\n\n# Load the two images\nimage1 = cv2.imread('image1.jpg')\nimage2 = cv2.imread('image2.jpg')\n\n# Compare the images\nsimilarity = compare_images(image1, image2)\n\n# Print the similarity value\nprint(similarity)"
"None\nTo detect if an image is blurry, you can use the following steps:\n\n1. Load the image using the OpenCV library.\n2. Calculate the mean and standard deviation of the pixel values in the image.\n3. Use a threshold to determine if the mean and standard deviation are significantly different from each other. If they are, it could indicate that the image is blurry.\n4. If the threshold is set to a certain value (e.g., 2 standard deviations), you can also check if the mean or standard deviation is above or below this value. If it is, it could indicate that the image is blurry.\n5. You can also use the Sobel operator or other edge detection techniques to detect edges in the image, which could help identify if the image is blurry due to lack of edges.\n\nHere's an example code snippet that demonstrates how to do this:\n```python\nimport cv2\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Load the image\nimage = cv2.imread('image.jpg')\n\n# Convert the image to grayscale\ngray = cv2.cvtColor(image, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)\n\n# Calculate the mean and standard deviation of the pixel values\nmean = np.mean(gray)\nstd_dev = np.std(gray)\n\n# Define a threshold for blurriness\nthreshold = 2\n\n# Check if the mean or standard deviation is significantly different from each other\nif mean > threshold or std_dev > threshold:\n    print(\"The image is blurry.\")\nelse:\n    print(\"The image is not blurry.\")\n```"
