"```python\nimport urllib.parse\n\n# Maximum lengths for each browser\nbrowser_max_lengths = {\n    \"Firefox\": 2048,  # Firefox has a maximum URL length of 2048 characters\n    \"Chrome\": 2048,  # Chrome has a maximum URL length of 2048 characters\n    \"Safari\": 2048,  # Safari has a maximum URL length of 2048 characters\n    \"Edge\": 2048,  # Edge has a maximum URL length of 2048 characters\n    \"Opera\": 2048  # Opera has a maximum URL length of 2048 characters\n}\n\ndef get_max_url_length(browser):\n    \"\"\"Returns the maximum length of a URL for the given browser.\"\"\"\n    return browser_max_lengths.get(browser, \"Maximum URL length not supported\")\n\ndef main():\n    print(\"Maximum Length of a URL in Different Browsers:\")\n    \n    # Get maximum URL length for each browser\n    for browser, max_length in browser_max_lengths.items():\n        print(f\"{browser}: {get_max_url_length(browser)}\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nThis Python code defines a dictionary `browser_max_lengths` to store the maximum lengths for each browser. It also includes a function `get_max_url_length` to retrieve the maximum URL length for a given browser. The `main` function iterates through the dictionary and prints the maximum URL length for each browser."
"**The Definitive Guide to Form-Based Website Authentication**\n\nTo implement form-based authentication on a website, you can use a combination of HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and a backend server-side language like Python. Here's a simple example of a form-based authentication system using Python and Flask:\n\n```python\nfrom flask import Flask, request, redirect, url_for\nfrom flask_session import Session\nfrom werkzeug.security import generate_password_hash, check_password_hash\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\napp.config[\"SESSION_PERMANENT\"] = False\napp.config[\"SESSION_TYPE\"] = \"filesystem\"\nSession(app)\n\n# In-memory storage for demonstration purposes only\nusers = {}\n\ndef create_user(username, password):\n    \"\"\"Create a new user\"\"\"\n    hashed_password = generate_password_hash(password)\n    users[username] = hashed_password\n    return hashed_password\n\ndef authenticate(username, password):\n    \"\"\"Check if a user exists and the password is correct\"\"\"\n    return username in users and check_password_hash(users[username], password)\n\ndef login():\n    \"\"\"Login function\"\"\"\n    username = request.form[\"username\"]\n    password = request.form[\"password\"]\n    if authenticate(username, password):\n        return redirect(url_for(\"protected\"))\n    else:\n        return \"Invalid username or password\"\n\n@app.route(\"/login\", methods=[\"GET\", \"POST\"])\ndef login_view():\n    if request.method == \"POST\":\n        username = request.form[\"username\"]\n        password = request.form[\"password\"]\n        if login():\n            return redirect(url_for(\"protected\"))\n    return render_template(\"login.html\")\n\n@app.route(\"/register\", methods=[\"GET\", \"POST\"])\ndef register_view():\n    if request.method == \"POST\":\n        username = request.form[\"username\"]\n        password = request.form[\"password\"]\n        if create_user(username, password):\n            return redirect(url_for(\"login\"))\n    return render_template(\"register.html\")\n\n@app.route(\"/protected\")\ndef protected():\n    \"\"\"Protected route\"\"\"\n    username = request.args.get(\"username\")\n    return render_template(\"protected.html\", username=username)\n\n@app.route(\"/logout\")\ndef logout():\n    \"\"\"Logout function\"\"\"\n    username = request.args.get(\"username\")\n    logout()\n    return redirect(url_for(\"login\"))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    app.run(debug=True)\n```\n\nThis code creates a simple form-based authentication system using Flask, in-memory storage, and a basic login function. The `login` function checks if the provided username and password match the stored credentials. The `create_user` function creates a new user with a hashed password. The `protected` function is a protected route that requires authentication to access."
"```python\nimport urllib.parse\n\ndef difference_between_uri_url_urn():\n    \"\"\"\n    This function demonstrates the difference between a URL, a URI, and a URN.\n    \n    A URL is a web address that can be typed into a web browser or used in a web application.\n    A URI is a more general term that refers to a Uniform Resource Identifier.\n    A URN is a Uniform Resource Name, which is a unique identifier for a resource.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # Create a URL object\n    url = urllib.parse.urlparse(\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uniform_Resource_Locator\")\n    \n    # Print the URL\n    print(\"URL:\", url)\n\n    # Create a URI object\n    uri = urllib.parse.quote(\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uniform_Resource_Locator\")\n    \n    # Print the URI\n    print(\"URI:\", uri)\n\n    # Create a URN object\n    urn = \"urn:isbn:0-486-27517-3\"\n    \n    # Print the URN\n    print(\"URN:\", urn)\n\n# Call the function\ndifference_between_uri_url_urn()"
"**What is RESTful Programming?**\n\nREST (Representational State of Resource) is an architectural style for designing networked applications. It's based on the idea of resources, which are identified by URIs, and can be manipulated using a fixed set of operations. This style is simple, scalable, and easy to implement, making it a popular choice for building web APIs and microservices.\n\n**Key Principles of RESTful Programming**\n\n- **Client-Server Architecture**: A client makes a request to a server, which responds with the requested data.\n- **Stateless**: Each request contains all the information necessary to complete the request, and the server does not maintain any session state.\n- **Cacheable**: Responses can be cached by the client to reduce the number of requests.\n- **Uniform Interface**: All client-server interactions use HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) and URL formatting.\n- **Layered System**: A RESTful API typically consists of multiple layers, including the client, server, and database.\n\n**Basic Concepts in RESTful Programming**\n\n- **Resource**: A single item or object, such as a user, product, or document.\n- **Resource Identifier (URI)**: A unique address that identifies a resource, such as `/users/123`.\n- **HTTP Methods**: Used to perform actions on resources, including:\n    - GET: Retrieve a resource\n    - POST: Create a new resource\n    - PUT: Update an existing resource\n    - DELETE: Delete a resource\n- **Status Codes**: Used to indicate the outcome of an HTTP request, including:\n    - 200 OK: The request was successful\n    - 400 Bad Request: The request was invalid\n    - 404 Not Found: The requested resource was not found\n\n**Example of a RESTful API**\n\nSuppose we have a simple API for managing books. The API would have the following endpoints:\n\n- `GET /books`: Retrieve a list of all books\n- `GET /books/{id}`: Retrieve a single book by ID\n- `POST /books`: Create a new book\n- `PUT /books/{id}`: Update an existing book\n- `DELETE /books/{id}`: Delete a book\n\nEach request would include a `GET /books` or `POST /books` method, followed by the necessary parameters (e.g., `id` or `title`). The server would respond with a `200 OK` status code, indicating that the request was successful."
