"```python\nimport socket\n\ndef connection_reset_by_peer_error():\n    \"\"\"\n    Explanation of the \"connection reset by peer\" error on a TCP connection.\n\n    The \"connection reset by peer\" error is a TCP error that occurs when a peer (i.e., the other end of the connection) closes the connection without any indication of what was being done. This can happen due to various reasons, including:\n\n    1.  Network failure or disconnection\n    2.  Insufficient resources (e.g., connection timeout, buffer overflow)\n    3.  Misconfiguration or mismatched protocol versions\n\n    This error is not a fatal error, but rather a notification that something has gone wrong. It can be handled by the application, and the connection can be re-established once the issue is resolved.\n\n    \"\"\"\n    # Create a socket object\n    sock = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)\n\n    # Set a timeout to avoid waiting indefinitely for a response\n    sock.settimeout(5)\n\n    # Attempt to establish a connection to a remote host\n    try:\n        # Try to connect to a remote host\n        sock.connect(('www.google.com', 80))\n\n        # If the connection is successful, print a success message\n        print(\"Connection established successfully.\")\n\n    # Catch the exception that occurs when the peer closes the connection\n    except socket.timeout:\n        # Print an error message indicating that the connection was reset by the peer\n        print(\"Connection reset by peer.\")\n\n    # Close the socket object\n    finally:\n        sock.close()\n\n# Call the function\nconnection_reset_by_peer_error()\n```"
"import socket\n\ndef explain_sooruetc():\n    # Explanation for SO_REUSEADDR\n    print(\"SO_REUSEADDR is a socket option that allows a socket to bind to a port that is already in use by another process.\")\n    print(\"It is similar to SO_REUSEPORT, but it only works on certain operating systems.\")\n    print(\"SO_REUSEADDR is not available on all operating systems, such as Windows and macOS.\")\n    print(\"On Unix-based systems, such as Linux and Solaris, it is available and works as expected.\")\n\n    # Explanation for SO_REUSEPORT\n    print(\"SO_REUSEPORT is a socket option that allows a socket to bind to a port that is already in use by another process, while also allowing the socket to listen for incoming connections.\")\n    print(\"It is a more advanced option than SO_REUSEADDR and is available on all Unix-based systems.\")\n    print(\"However, it is not available on some operating systems, such as Windows.\")\n\n    # Answer to the questions\n    print(\"\\nAnswer to the questions:\")\n    print(\"SO_REUSEADDR is similar to SO_REUSEPORT, but it only works on certain operating systems.\")\n    print(\"SO_REUSEADDR is not available on all operating systems, while SO_REUSEPORT is available on all Unix-based systems.\")\n    print(\"If you use SO_REUSEADDR on a system without SO_REUSEPORT, the socket may not be able to bind to the port.\")\n\nexplain_sooruetc()"
