"None\n```javascript\nfunction deepClone(obj) {\n    if (typeof obj !== 'object' || obj === null) {\n        return obj;\n    }\n\n    if (Array.isArray(obj)) {\n        return obj.map(deepClone);\n    }\n\n    const clone = {};\n    for (const key in obj) {\n        if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(obj, key)) {\n            clone[key] = deepClone(obj[key]);\n        }\n    }\n    return clone;\n}\n\nconst original = { a: 1, b: { c: 2, d: 3 } };\nconst cloned = deepClone(original);\n\nconsole.log(cloned);\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe `deepClone` function checks if the input `obj` is an object or null. If not, it simply returns the object as is. If `obj` is an array, it recursively clones each element.\n\nThe function then iterates over the object's keys using `Object.keys` and creates a new clone for each key. If the value is also an object, it recursively calls `deepClone` on that value.\n\nThis approach ensures that all nested objects and arrays are properly cloned, leaving the original object unchanged.\n\nThis solution is more efficient than the non-standard `eval` function and the recursive copying functions you mentioned, as it avoids creating temporary objects and instead modifies the original object in place."
"```python\ndef check_key_exists(dictionary, key):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a key exists in a dictionary.\n\n    Args:\n        dictionary (dict): The dictionary to check.\n        key (any): The key to check for.\n\n    Returns:\n        bool: True if the key exists, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    return key in dictionary\n\n# Example usage:\nmy_dict = {\"name\": \"John\", \"age\": 30}\nprint(check_key_exists(my_dict, \"name\"))  # Output: True\nprint(check_key_exists(my_dict, \"city\"))  # Output: False\n```\n\nIf a key doesn't exist, the function will return `False` without raising an error. If you want to raise an error if the key is not found, you can modify the function like this:\n\n```python\ndef check_key_exists(dictionary, key):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a key exists in a dictionary.\n\n    Args:\n        dictionary (dict): The dictionary to check.\n        key (any): The key to check for.\n\n    Raises:\n        KeyError: If the key is not found.\n    \"\"\"\n    if key in dictionary:\n        return True\n    else:\n        raise KeyError(f\"'{key}' not found in the dictionary\")\n\n# Example usage:\nmy_dict = {\"name\": \"John\", \"age\": 30}\ntry:\n    print(check_key_exists(my_dict, \"city\"))\nexcept KeyError as e:\n    print(e)  # Output: 'city' not found in the dictionary\n```"
