"None\nYou can pass an enum value as a command parameter in WPF by using the `EnumMember` class to specify the enum member that will be passed as the command parameter. Here's an example:\n\n```xaml\n<Window x:Class=\"WpfApp1.MainWindow\"\n        xmlns=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation\"\n        xmlns:x=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml\"\n        Title=\"MainWindow\" Height=\"350\" Width=\"525\">\n  <Grid>\n    <Button x:Name=\"uxSearchButton\" Command=\"{Binding Path=SearchMembersCommand}\" \n            CommandParameter=\"{Binding Path=SearchPageType.First}\" \n            Content=\"Search\">\n    </Button>\n  </Grid>\n</Window>\n```\n\nAnd in your code-behind:\n\n```csharp\npublic partial class MainWindow : Window\n{\n    public enum SearchPageType\n    {\n        First,\n        Second,\n        Third\n    }\n\n    private void SearchMembersCommand(object sender, RoutedEventArgs e)\n    {\n        Console.WriteLine(SearchPageType(sender.ToString()));\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the `SearchPageType.First` enum member will be passed as the command parameter when the \"Search\" button is clicked."
"```python\nimport tkinter as tk\n\nclass OverlayControl:\n    def __init__(self, root):\n        self.root = root\n        self.root.overrideredirect(True)\n\n        # Create the overlay control\n        self.overlay = tk.Frame(root)\n        self.overlay.pack(fill=\"both\", expand=True)\n\n        # Create the main frame\n        self.main_frame = tk.Frame(root)\n        self.main_frame.pack(fill=\"both\", expand=True)\n\n    def run(self):\n        self.root.mainloop()\n\n# Create the root window\nroot = tk.Tk()\n\n# Create an instance of the OverlayControl\noverlay_control = OverlayControl(root)\n\n# Run the OverlayControl\noverlay_control.run()\n\n# Close the root window\nroot.destroy()\n```\n\nThis code creates a simple overlay control that appears above all other controls in the window. The `overrideredirect(True)` method is used to disable the window's title bar and other menu items, allowing the overlay control to take priority. The overlay control is then packed into the main frame, which is also packed into the root window."
