"None\n \nYou are trying to use the 'if' statement with a boolean value directly. In Python, 'True' is a boolean value that can be used directly in an 'if' statement. The 'if' statement will only execute the code inside it if the condition is met (i.e., if the boolean value is 'True'). If the condition is not met, the code will not be executed. \n\nHowever, the syntax you're trying to use is a bit unusual. In Python, you can't directly use a boolean value as a condition in an 'if' statement like this. You need to convert the boolean value to a string or an integer that can be compared with the boolean value. \n\nFor example:\n\n```python\na = 100\nb = True\nprint(a if b else 'Not a boolean value')\n```\n\nThis code will print \"Not a boolean value\" if 'a' is not a boolean value, and '100' if 'a' is a boolean value. \n\nIf you want to print 'a' only when 'b' is True, you can use the ternary operator:\n\n```python\na = 100\nb = True\nprint(a if b else 'Not a boolean value')\n```"
