"```python\nimport smtplib\nfrom email.mime.multipart import MIMEMultipart\nfrom email.mime.text import MIMEText\n\ndef send_email_attachment(subject, message, from_addr, to_addr, password, attachment_path):\n    # Create a message object\n    msg = MIMEMultipart()\n\n    # Set the sender and receiver\n    msg['From'] = from_addr\n    msg['To'] = to_addr\n\n    # Set the subject\n    msg['Subject'] = subject\n\n    # Attach the file\n    attachment = open(attachment_path, 'rb')\n    part = MIMEAttachment('attachment', filename=attachment_path, content_type='application/octet-stream')\n    part.add_header('Content-Disposition', 'attachment', filename=attachment_path)\n    msg.attach(part)\n\n    # Send the email\n    server = smtplib.SMTP('smtp.gmail.com', 587)\n    server.starttls()\n    server.login(from_addr, password)\n    text = msg.as_string()\n    server.sendmail(from_addr, to_addr, text)\n    server.quit()\n\n# Example usage\nsubject = \"Email with attachment\"\nmessage = \"This is a test email with an attachment.\"\nfrom_addr = \"your-email@gmail.com\"\nto_addr = \"recipient-email@example.com\"\npassword = \"your-password\"\n\nattachment_path = \"/path/to/your/attachment.txt\"\nsend_email_attachment(subject, message, from_addr, to_addr, password, attachment_path)\n```"
"None\nimport smtplib\nfrom email.mime.text import MIMEText\n\ndef send_html_email(to_email, from_email, subject, content):\n    # Define the SMTP server details\n    smtp_server = \"smtp.gmail.com\"\n    smtp_port = 587\n    smtp_user = \"your-email@gmail.com\"\n    smtp_pass = \"your-password\"\n\n    # Create a message\n    msg = MIMEText(content)\n    msg['Subject'] = subject\n    msg['From'] = from_email\n    msg['To'] = to_email\n\n    # Set up the SMTP server\n    server = smtplib.SMTP(smtp_server, smtp_port)\n    server.starttls()\n    server.login(smtp_user, smtp_pass)\n\n    # Send the email\n    server.sendmail(from_email, to_email, msg.as_string())\n    server.quit()\n\n# Example usage:\nsend_html_email(\"recipient-email@example.com\", \"sender-email@example.com\", \"Test Email\", \"<p>This is a test email.</p>\")"
"To use local Docker images with Minikube, you can create a Dockerfile in your local repository and then load the images into Minikube using the `eval $(minikube docker-env)` command. Here's an example Dockerfile:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM fluxcapacitor/jupyterhub:latest\n\n# Set the working directory in the container\nWORKDIR /app\n\n# Copy the current directory contents into the container at /app\nCOPY . /app\n\n# Install the dependencies\nRUN pip install -r requirements.txt\n\n# Expose the port for the JupyterHub service\nEXPOSE 8888\n\n# Run the JupyterHub service\nCMD [\"jupyterhub\", \"--port\", \"8888\"]\n```\n\nThis Dockerfile assumes you have a `requirements.txt` file in your local repository with the following content:\n\n```makefile\nhdfs\nzeppelin\nprediction-pmml\nscheduler-airflow\nloadtest\nhdfs\nsql-mysql\nmetastore-1.2.1\ncassandra\napachespark-worker-2.0.1\napachespark-master-2.0.1\npackage-java-openjdk-1.8\nstream-kafka-0.10\npipeline\ngcr.io/google_containers/kube-addon-manager\ngcr.io/google_containers/kubedns-amd64\ngcr.io/google_containers/kube-dnsmasq-amd64\ngcr.io/google_containers/exechealthz-amd64\ngcr.io/google_containers/pause-amd64\n```\n\nTo load the images into Minikube, run the following command:\n\n```bash\neval $(minikube docker-env)\n```\n\nThis will load the local Docker images into Minikube. You can then start the Minikube cluster and access the JupyterHub service by navigating to `http://localhost:8888`."
"# Possible Causes for Pods Stuck in Terminating Status\n\nWhen a ReplicationController (RC) in a Kubernetes cluster is stuck in Terminating status, it typically indicates that the controller is experiencing issues with the pods it is managing. Here are some possible causes for this issue:\n\n- Insufficient resources (e.g., CPU or memory) in the control plane node or worker nodes, causing the RC to fail to create new pods.\n- Incompatible pod specifications (e.g., incorrect image or container port numbers) that prevent the RC from scaling correctly.\n- Inconsistent pod scheduling or priority levels, leading to uneven distribution of resources among pods.\n- Overheating or resource-intensive pods that consume excessive resources, causing the RC to fail to scale correctly.\n- Misconfigured ReplicationController configuration, such as incorrect replicas or selector, that prevents the RC from managing the desired number of pods.\n\nTo troubleshoot this issue, it is essential to monitor the pods and the ReplicationController logs for any clues that may indicate the root cause."
