"None\nThe convention of using `/healthz` for application health checks in the Kubernetes/Docker ecosystem originates from the concept of \"health\" as a measure of the system's overall condition. In this context, a healthy system is one that is functioning correctly and responding to requests. The `/healthz` endpoint is essentially a health check that monitors the system's status and reports back to clients if it detects any issues.\n\nThe name \"healthz\" is thought to come from the idea of checking for \"health\" in a literal sense, rather than a more abstract concept. In the past, health checks were often performed using simple commands or scripts that would execute a specific set of checks to determine the system's status. The `/healthz` endpoint is a modern extension of this concept, where the checks are performed using a standardized protocol (HTTP 200 OK response with a 200 status code) and a specific set of HTTP methods (GET, HEAD, and OPTIONS)."
"Here is the Python code that generates the Kubernetes service external IP:\n\n```python\nimport os\nimport requests\n\ndef get_service_ip():\n    try:\n        response = requests.get('https://kubernetes.io/v1/1.16/capability-set-internal/external-ips.json')\n        response.raise_for_status()\n        data = response.json()\n        service_ip = data['items'][0]['externalIPs'][0]['ip']\n        return service_ip\n    except requests.exceptions.RequestException as err:\n        print(f\"Request Exception: {err}\")\n        return None\n\ndef main():\n    service_ip = get_service_ip()\n    if service_ip is None:\n        return\n\n    print(f\"Kubernetes Service External IP: {service_ip}\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```"
